WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.893 --> 00:00:02.588
Stuck always feels the same.

2
00:00:03.309 --> 00:00:06.479
Yet there are four kinds, each with its own fix.

3
00:00:07.539 --> 00:00:10.024
Unclear goal: no agreement on success.

4
00:00:10.736 --> 00:00:14.253
Unclear way: the goal is clear, nobody knows how.

5
00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:18.618
People problem: someone whose yes you need isn't giving it.

6
00:00:19.351 --> 00:00:22.672
Capacity problem: everyone agrees, nobody has time.

7
00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:26.897
Four questions: same goal for sponsor, owner and team?

8
00:00:27.617 --> 00:00:28.859
Next three steps?

9
00:00:29.557 --> 00:00:30.463
Support we lack?

10
00:00:31.186 --> 00:00:31.952
Real hours?

11
00:00:32.951 --> 00:00:35.389
The first no is usually where you start.

12
00:00:36.732 --> 00:00:41.516
Goal: long debates, shifting sponsor, every workshop reopening the last.

13
00:00:42.592 --> 00:00:45.355
Way: analysis loops, waiting for data.

14
00:00:46.091 --> 00:00:48.680
People: one person slips, silence.

15
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:54.374
Capacity: everyone slips, meetings get cancelled, work in the evenings.

16
00:00:55.365 --> 00:00:59.068
Fixes: the sponsor chooses, an experiment or an expert,

17
00:00:59.254 --> 00:01:01.959
a one-on-one, and a trade-off by the sponsor.

18
00:01:03.015 --> 00:01:07.462
Classic mistake: a goal problem treated as a people one, pushing harder.

19
00:01:08.154 --> 00:01:09.187
Two kinds at once?

20
00:01:09.892 --> 00:01:11.250
Start with the earliest.

21
00:01:12.288 --> 00:01:13.542
Two weeks about to act?

22
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.620
See it in week three, not week nine.

23
00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:21.126
Experts argue: always people, or a decision nobody took?

24
00:01:21.886 --> 00:01:24.115
Ask: which decision is nobody taking?

25
00:01:25.447 --> 00:01:29.464
Week 7, grid operator: third workshop on a readiness check.

26
00:01:30.203 --> 00:01:31.666
Is a job ready for the crew?

27
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.912
Planning wants strict:

28
00:01:34.074 --> 00:01:37.755
nothing released without a confirmed switching plan and material.

29
00:01:38.461 --> 00:01:40.063
Execution wants light.

30
00:01:41.126 --> 00:01:43.320
The sponsor said: just make it work.

31
00:01:44.098 --> 00:01:46.663
The team tires, but the goal is unclear.

32
00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:51.398
Strict serves fewer cancellations, light faster release.

33
00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:54.232
Another workshop won't settle that.

34
00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:59.236
Write one page with both goals and what each design does.

35
00:01:59.946 --> 00:02:01.479
Tell both heads beforehand.

36
00:02:02.546 --> 00:02:07.400
The sponsor chooses, or sets one target: cancellations under 8 percent,

37
00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.547
release at most 2 days longer.

38
00:02:10.876 --> 00:02:13.501
What does being wrong cost, and can you undo it?

39
00:02:14.555 --> 00:02:19.013
A reversible decision, like a planning rule, you make fast, and test.

40
00:02:20.062 --> 00:02:21.490
An irreversible decision,

41
00:02:21.699 --> 00:02:25.565
like buying a machine or a reorganisation: slow and careful.

42
00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.853
Jeff Bezos called reversible decisions two-way doors:

43
00:02:29.946 --> 00:02:32.094
small groups should make them fast.

44
00:02:33.106 --> 00:02:36.322
Critics: even small is socially hard to undo.

45
00:02:37.001 --> 00:02:39.752
What does going back cost, in money and trust?

46
00:02:41.096 --> 00:02:44.765
An experiment: a small test, time-boxed, with a measure.

47
00:02:45.481 --> 00:02:48.766
Start from a hypothesis, a guess a test can prove wrong.

48
00:02:49.792 --> 00:02:52.741
If planners confirm ahead, do cancellations drop?

49
00:02:53.458 --> 00:02:57.081
Test small: two planners, two weeks, about 40 jobs.

50
00:02:58.114 --> 00:03:02.921
Agree first what you measure, and the stop criterion: when to go on, when to stop.

51
00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:07.590
Two weeks, then decide together: easier to say yes to.

52
00:03:08.376 --> 00:03:09.409
Then really stop.

53
00:03:10.428 --> 00:03:14.004
Failures: no comparison group, two changes at once,

54
00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.222
or a test team with all the attention.

55
00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:20.295
Three weeks of debate: calling the contractor ahead,

56
00:03:20.435 --> 00:03:22.560
planner time or fewer cancellations?

57
00:03:23.335 --> 00:03:25.703
No data; 16 percent cancelled.

58
00:03:26.789 --> 00:03:28.902
Reversible and cheap: test it.

59
00:03:29.649 --> 00:03:31.657
Two planners call; the rest don't.

60
00:03:32.737 --> 00:03:36.916
A third fewer, about 11 percent, and calls under 10 minutes?

61
00:03:37.631 --> 00:03:39.175
Roll out; else stop.

62
00:03:40.255 --> 00:03:45.700
At 6 minutes, 40 calls cost 4 hours, small next to a cancelled crew day.

63
00:03:46.409 --> 00:03:48.255
Call it a test; share the result.

64
00:03:49.356 --> 00:03:53.745
Never experiment with safety: not switching procedures, not lockout.

65
00:03:54.885 --> 00:03:59.146
Nor the five safety rules: disconnect, secure against reconnection,

66
00:03:59.296 --> 00:04:02.861
verify absence of voltage, earth and short-circuit,

67
00:04:02.977 --> 00:04:05.345
protect against adjacent live parts.

68
00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:09.422
Projects rarely fail suddenly, they drift.

69
00:04:10.196 --> 00:04:13.296
Hoping to catch up halfway is the costliest choice.

70
00:04:14.322 --> 00:04:17.550
Sunk cost: what is spent feels like a reason to go on.

71
00:04:18.308 --> 00:04:19.852
Only the future counts.

72
00:04:20.912 --> 00:04:23.374
A reset: look honestly, a new deal.

73
00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:27.881
First one page: plan versus reality, and causes.

74
00:04:28.917 --> 00:04:31.924
A RAG status, red, amber or green,

75
00:04:32.098 --> 00:04:35.175
green for weeks while the goal slips: a watermelon.

76
00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:40.769
Name your own part: I should have escalated the capacity issue in week four.

77
00:04:42.094 --> 00:04:46.401
Your manager first, then the sponsor, 45 minutes: as set up,

78
00:04:46.587 --> 00:04:48.561
this project won't reach its goal.

79
00:04:49.566 --> 00:04:54.210
With what you see, the cause and three options: stop and hand over what works,

80
00:04:54.407 --> 00:04:56.439
shrink and finish, or re-plan.

81
00:04:57.499 --> 00:05:03.210
Re-contracting: goal, scope, end date, roles, the sponsor's commitments, in writing.

82
00:05:04.219 --> 00:05:08.143
Plus a check-in point: not at X by week 20, we stop.

83
00:05:08.872 --> 00:05:11.008
Only moving dates fails again.

84
00:05:12.057 --> 00:05:12.846
Uncomfortable?

85
00:05:13.523 --> 00:05:16.228
Do it anyway; sponsors are often relieved.

86
00:05:17.007 --> 00:05:19.399
Knowing what we know, would we start this today?

87
00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:25.191
Week 14 of 20: release twice as fast, in three regions.

88
00:05:25.913 --> 00:05:28.966
The pilot: one region, 10 percent faster.

89
00:05:30.097 --> 00:05:33.964
The sponsor came to one of four steering groups, two planners left.

90
00:05:34.717 --> 00:05:36.319
Yet the team reports amber.

91
00:05:37.042 --> 00:05:38.853
Cause: capacity and people.

92
00:05:39.848 --> 00:05:40.545
A: stop.

93
00:05:41.288 --> 00:05:45.200
B: the pilot region, 40 percent shorter, six weeks.

94
00:05:45.915 --> 00:05:48.562
C: three regions, ten weeks later.

95
00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.820
Recommend B, unless she truly commits to C.

96
00:05:53.521 --> 00:05:55.668
Write it down, with a check-in point.
